WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.500 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.728 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.728 --> 00:00:20.102 align:middle line:90%
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:00:20.102 --> 00:00:41.560 align:middle line:90%


00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.560 align:middle line:84%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK, welcome
back to the second half

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.530 align:middle line:90%
of the second lecture.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.620 align:middle line:84%
Here, we're going to take
this beautiful example

00:00:53.620 --> 00:01:02.140 align:middle line:84%
of an algorithm, which the
cellular machinery applies

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.190 align:middle line:90%
to get from DNA to RNA protein.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:10.780 align:middle line:84%
And so a simple
example where that

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.440 align:middle line:84%
is incorporated into a program
very similar to what you'll

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.520 align:middle line:90%
be doing on your problem set.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.750 align:middle line:84%
So here, we've got it
nicely color coded.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.010 align:middle line:84%
This is unrelated, of
course, to the color coding

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.230 align:middle line:84%
and the genetic code
we've been using so far,

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.560 align:middle line:84%
but it includes
the genetic code.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.280 align:middle line:84%
You can see the comments
here are preceded

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.340 align:middle line:90%
by this little number sign.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.720 align:middle line:84%
We go from the genome, a DNA
sequence, which is a string.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.930 align:middle line:84%
Strings are one of the things
that Perl does very well.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.630 align:middle line:84%
It's not the only
string-manipulation programming

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.790 align:middle line:84%
language, but it's a
particularly easy one.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.340 align:middle line:84%
And so here is how
easy it is to enter

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.960 align:middle line:84%
a DNA sequence, one of the
many ways of entering a DNA

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.460 align:middle line:90%
sequence.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:55.793 align:middle line:90%
You can bring it in from a file.

00:01:55.793 --> 00:01:58.390 align:middle line:90%
Here it is as part of the code.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.790 align:middle line:84%
You transcribe it into
RNA in silico here,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.000 align:middle line:84%
by the simple
command where you say

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.539 align:middle line:84%
RNA sequence is equal
to DNA sequence,

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:16.480 align:middle line:84%
and then you substitute
all the Ts for Us globally.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.550 align:middle line:84%
That's what this 12th
line is on slide 23.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, off to the side
here, is a reminder

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.670 align:middle line:84%
that it's really much more
complicated than that.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.030 align:middle line:84%
There's all these proteins
involved and doing it

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.100 align:middle line:84%
accurately and with
regulations, so forth.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.710 align:middle line:84%
But for the sake of
this Perl program,

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:36.490 align:middle line:84%
this is quite sufficient to
get us to an RNA sequence,

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:42.040 align:middle line:84%
which we can then translate, and
here, the translation process

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.440 align:middle line:90%
uses--

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.140 align:middle line:90%
it's going to be a cycle.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.190 align:middle line:84%
So in a sense, the
cycle in the RNA m

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:53.180 align:middle line:84%
where you put in one nucleotide
at a m is all compact here.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.360 align:middle line:84%
It's all just substitute
every T for U.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.500 align:middle line:84%
Here, we're going to have a more
explicit loop, this while loop.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.730 align:middle line:90%
You can see it's indented.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.310 align:middle line:84%
Everything within the loop,
that's going to be iterated,

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.293 align:middle line:90%
is offset a bit.

00:03:04.293 --> 00:03:05.710 align:middle line:84%
And what you're
going to be doing,

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.900 align:middle line:90%
is looking in groups of 3.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.550 align:middle line:84%
That's what this
3 is on line 17 is

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.970 align:middle line:84%
looking through the
position you are in the RNA,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.130 align:middle line:84%
and taking a chunk
of 3 at a time.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.280 align:middle line:84%
The chunk here is a
substring, substring.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.860 align:middle line:84%
And you pull out a
codon as that substring.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.490 align:middle line:90%
And then you do a translation.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.250 align:middle line:84%
So now this is, in a
sense, representing

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.560 align:middle line:84%
the modularity of biology
and the modularity

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:33.880 align:middle line:90%
of good programming code.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:39.160 align:middle line:84%
It's you put this whole
business of translating

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.790 align:middle line:84%
in a separate part
of the program,

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.600 align:middle line:84%
so you don't have to
embed the code everywhere

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.800 align:middle line:90%
that it's going to be used.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.140 align:middle line:84%
And the translation here,
is that simple table.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.560 align:middle line:84%
So this subroutine,
S-U-B, line 22, again,

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.410 align:middle line:84%
indented all the code
in the subroutine.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.660 align:middle line:84%
It goes off the
bottom of the slide

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:00.370 align:middle line:84%
and down through the floor
all the different cases.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, we could list 64 cases
for the 64 trinucleotides,

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.490 align:middle line:84%
or we can use the more
compact string manipulation

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.230 align:middle line:84%
that you can do in
Perl to indicate--

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.950 align:middle line:84%
a dot means any kind of
character after G and C,

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:17.440 align:middle line:84%
would return alanine
so GC, ACG, or U.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.740 align:middle line:84%
And then for cysteine,
which has two possibilities,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.060 align:middle line:84%
so that's four possibilities
represented by that dot.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.140 align:middle line:84%
And cysteine has
two possibilities.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.610 align:middle line:84%
It's either UGC, or UGU, where
the vertical line means or.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:28.550 align:middle line:90%
And you get the idea.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.570 align:middle line:84%
There's a whole,
very compact, syntax

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:35.470 align:middle line:90%
here for doing the translation.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:43.210 align:middle line:84%
And that's how we do one of the
cleaner, more simple algorithms

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.190 align:middle line:90%
in computational biology.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.660 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to make
it complicated again.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:55.300 align:middle line:84%
But first, to set the stage
for how the genetic code is not

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.420 align:middle line:84%
universal, we have to
explain by what we might

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.100 align:middle line:90%
mean by it being universal.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:06.130 align:middle line:84%
This is the ultimate
pedigree on slide 24 here.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:10.480 align:middle line:84%
It's, in principle, some
very simple organism,

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.600 align:middle line:84%
possibly an RNA-based
organism that

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.960 align:middle line:84%
may have made a
proto-ribosome that

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.180 align:middle line:84%
may have done protein
synthesis or may have done

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:23.620 align:middle line:84%
some other chemical reaction
at what is now [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.670 align:middle line:90%
transferase site.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.120 align:middle line:84%
Something along this line, it's
speculated by some biologists,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.980 align:middle line:84%
was the common ancestor of
all living species, all living

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.000 align:middle line:90%
cells.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.180 align:middle line:84%
And certainly, by
the time we started

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.070 align:middle line:84%
getting branching of
the three major branches

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:45.040 align:middle line:84%
bacteria, the archaea bacteria,
and the eukaryotes, by the time

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.750 align:middle line:84%
we get to that point,
there was, probably

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:53.710 align:middle line:84%
a ribosome set of genes that
encodes all the proteins

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.260 align:middle line:84%
and RNAs or the ribosome,
and these were shared.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.838 align:middle line:84%
And then at each
cell division, you

00:05:59.838 --> 00:06:01.630 align:middle line:84%
split off two cells
were slightly different

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:02.338 align:middle line:90%
from one another.

00:06:02.338 --> 00:06:06.190 align:middle line:84%
As they would mutate and
differentiate and be selected,

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.920 align:middle line:84%
they would generate this
huge diversity of organisms,

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:10.520 align:middle line:90%
which you see here.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is a [? directed ?]
basically graph in the sense

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:17.980 align:middle line:84%
that you can't have a
descendant in this process

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:22.780 align:middle line:84%
being an ancestor of
one of its parents.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:27.920 align:middle line:84%
So time has, as an axis,
going up in this case,

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.210 align:middle line:84%
unlike some of the more physics
based diagrams we had before.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:36.040 align:middle line:84%
And as you branch out
to existing species,

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:40.900 align:middle line:84%
you see that things like plants,
have actually inheritance,

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:45.160 align:middle line:84%
not just along this direct
tree-like structure,

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.130 align:middle line:84%
but you've got more of a
network-like structure, where

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:49.660 align:middle line:84%
some of the genomic
material came

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:53.110 align:middle line:84%
in from one of the
bacterial branches long ago.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.700 align:middle line:84%
And this has recently been
put on very firm footing

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.990 align:middle line:84%
on a genomic scale, in a
recent article this month,

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.110 align:middle line:84%
where the bacterial genome
of cyanobacteria, these

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:08.050 align:middle line:84%
are blue-green algae
that fix carbon

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.010 align:middle line:90%
in all the oceans and plant.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.110 align:middle line:84%
And the simplest plant
that's been sequenced

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.650 align:middle line:90%
is Arabidopsis, a weed.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:20.245 align:middle line:84%
And anyway, the DNA
from the bacteria

00:07:20.245 --> 00:07:22.120 align:middle line:84%
has not only gone into
the chloroplast, which

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.390 align:middle line:84%
is an organelle, which
has a very reduced genome,

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:27.670 align:middle line:84%
but it's been spread
through thousands of genes

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.460 align:middle line:84%
in the nucleus, which
is the major place where

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.810 align:middle line:84%
all the chromosomes
are for the plants.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:38.860 align:middle line:84%
And so this, possibly
symbiotic relationship,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.050 align:middle line:84%
has resulted in a
complicated inheritance.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:43.780 align:middle line:90%
And this is not unique.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.970 align:middle line:84%
Another one coming in
from the purple bacteria,

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.190 align:middle line:84%
has been incorporated into
a separate membrane-bound

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.840 align:middle line:84%
organelle, which provides
the ATP for both plants

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.890 align:middle line:84%
and non-photosynthetic
multicellular

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:58.780 align:middle line:90%
organisms, animals.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.260 align:middle line:84%
And these two
arrows, that could be

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:05.590 align:middle line:84%
there could be thousands
of arrows going

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.170 align:middle line:84%
over long periods of
time at deep branches,

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:10.690 align:middle line:84%
and we know there are
many interconnecting

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:14.560 align:middle line:84%
arrows in recent times in
almost all these organisms,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.500 align:middle line:84%
or certain representatives
from all over this tree,

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:20.980 align:middle line:84%
can take up DNA in
various ways, can even

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.580 align:middle line:84%
mate with organisms of various
species and exchange DNA

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:25.840 align:middle line:90%
and incorporate it.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.090 align:middle line:84%
And so it's not
this simple tree,

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:33.610 align:middle line:84%
but it is, certainly,
going forward in time.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:39.210 align:middle line:84%
It is directed and
acyclic in that regard.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:40.789 align:middle line:84%
So how many living
species are there?

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:43.070 align:middle line:84%
We're building to the
point, connecting back

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:46.670 align:middle line:84%
to the central dogma, of how
many different genetic codes

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:47.280 align:middle line:90%
are there.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.310 align:middle line:84%
So we need to know how
many species there are.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:56.600 align:middle line:84%
If you take a gram of
some thumbnail of soil,

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.000 align:middle line:84%
from any of a variety
of different soils

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.770 align:middle line:84%
that have been
tested, you can find

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.283 align:middle line:90%
about 5,000 bacterial species.

00:09:02.283 --> 00:09:03.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, what does it
mean to be a species?

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:06.020 align:middle line:84%
In animals, that
typically means that they

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.630 align:middle line:84%
don't produce fertile
offspring when they interbreed

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:09.740 align:middle line:90%
two different species.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:12.320 align:middle line:84%
But there are books full
of exceptions of this,

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:13.080 align:middle line:90%
even in animals.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.080 align:middle line:84%
And bacteria, of course,
where they exchange DNA

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:17.840 align:middle line:84%
all over the place, as I said,
it breaks down even more.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.980 align:middle line:84%
So working definition that
many biologists adopt,

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:27.950 align:middle line:84%
is that if two microbial
species share 20% of their DNA,

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.350 align:middle line:84%
if you take their DNA and
align it by algorithms,

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:33.080 align:middle line:84%
such as the ones we we'll
be using this course,

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.750 align:middle line:84%
and you find that 70% of the
base pairs are conserved,

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.030 align:middle line:90%
then they're the same species.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:40.700 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, they're
different species.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.160 align:middle line:84%
And there are millions
of non-microbial species,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.090 align:middle line:84%
many of which harbor
microbial species.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:53.420 align:middle line:84%
This number is dropping slightly
because of our inability

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:58.610 align:middle line:84%
to restrain our growth
and other activities

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:04.250 align:middle line:90%
that cause species extinction.

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.880 align:middle line:84%
And the number of whole genomes
is getting closer to 100,

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:12.620 align:middle line:84%
and the number in the pipeline
is probably 600 or so,

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:15.800 align:middle line:84%
maybe in the thousands,
with new technologies.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:20.540 align:middle line:84%
And there's over
80,000 species defined

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:24.620 align:middle line:84%
by one or more nucleic acid
sequences in the NCBI, National

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.650 align:middle line:84%
Center for
Bioinformatics, which is

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:31.173 align:middle line:84%
one of the three major
nucleic-acid databases

00:10:31.173 --> 00:10:31.715 align:middle line:90%
in the world.

00:10:31.715 --> 00:10:36.170 align:middle line:90%


00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.900 align:middle line:84%
Why do we study more
than one species?

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:43.660 align:middle line:84%
The comparison between species
allows subtle and not so subtle

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:47.530 align:middle line:84%
analyses of what are
the important positions

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:51.130 align:middle line:84%
to stay constant because they
provide some very fundamental

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.690 align:middle line:90%
biochemical activity.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:55.660 align:middle line:84%
But what are the important ones
to vary because they provide

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:57.580 align:middle line:84%
some important
variants, for example,

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.443 align:middle line:84%
escaping immune
surveillance and so on.

00:11:00.443 --> 00:11:01.860 align:middle line:84%
So there's reasons
to be constant,

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.820 align:middle line:84%
there's reason to be variable,
and reasons to be neutral.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:06.910 align:middle line:84%
So let's go back
now and apply this

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.500 align:middle line:84%
to the genetic code,
this particularly

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.260 align:middle line:84%
simple and elegant,
nearly-universal code.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.920 align:middle line:84%
This is how genetic
codes are represented

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.690 align:middle line:90%
in CBI, one of the ways.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.420 align:middle line:84%
And here, the three
bases of the codon,

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:22.060 align:middle line:84%
bases 1, 2, and 3
remember, we said

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:26.080 align:middle line:84%
that you UUU was phenylalanine
encoded by DNA TTT.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.410 align:middle line:84%
So going down from the top
on the leftmost column,

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:33.740 align:middle line:84%
it's TTT single letter
code F for phenylalanine.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:40.190 align:middle line:84%
So the amino acids go along
the bottom row of this table.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:44.110 align:middle line:84%
And you can see all the
amino acids are represented.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:45.910 align:middle line:84%
Stars represent
stop codons which

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:47.620 align:middle line:84%
are not recognized
by transfer RNAs

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:50.020 align:middle line:84%
but by proteins called
release factors that

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.970 align:middle line:84%
simulate the function
of the transfer RNA

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:56.410 align:middle line:84%
and cause the release of
this cyclic incorporation

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:58.450 align:middle line:84%
of amino acids
into polypeptides.

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:01.290 align:middle line:90%


00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:05.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is the
standard code, so-called,

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:08.790 align:middle line:84%
where you have one
methionine here in the middle

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:15.420 align:middle line:84%
here, encoded by ATG, and three
stop codons in all the rest.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.230 align:middle line:84%
Here's where it
gets complicated.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:22.440 align:middle line:84%
There are over 22
different genetic codes.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.300 align:middle line:84%
Some of the changes
from the standard code

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.390 align:middle line:90%
are indicated here in blue.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.910 align:middle line:84%
You have here, for example,
for the [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:32.430 align:middle line:84%
mitochondrial code, this
is the code actually used

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:35.580 align:middle line:84%
in every cell in your body for
the subset of the cell that

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.760 align:middle line:84%
is the powerhouse that makes
ATP, the mitochondria that we

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.830 align:middle line:84%
talked about before,
which was part

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.920 align:middle line:84%
of the horizontal
transmission of information

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.380 align:middle line:90%
from purple bacteria long ago.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:50.430 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, the normal stop
codon is now tryptophan,

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:54.570 align:middle line:84%
abbreviated W. There's
an extra methionine.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.142 align:middle line:84%
And there's two
extra stop codons,

00:12:56.142 --> 00:12:57.600 align:middle line:84%
which are replacing
what would have

00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:59.257 align:middle line:84%
been an arginine in
the standard code.

00:12:59.257 --> 00:13:01.590 align:middle line:84%
And you can see there's little
blues all over the place.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.415 align:middle line:84%
Changes in the number
where you can start.

00:13:05.415 --> 00:13:08.760 align:middle line:84%
The start sites that
are indicated by 3,

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:11.370 align:middle line:84%
1 to 3 starts in
the standard code.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And when you talk about the
starts, you start getting to

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:22.800 align:middle line:84%
how much do you favor
that particular start?

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:25.443 align:middle line:84%
What other signals
are required to start

00:13:25.443 --> 00:13:26.610 align:middle line:90%
at that particular position?

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:27.990 align:middle line:84%
It's not as simple
as just having

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:32.610 align:middle line:84%
an ATG trinucleotide to get
a start of protein synthesis.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:36.240 align:middle line:84%
You need other nucleic
acid components.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:41.460 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, still, it's a slightly
more complicated algorithm.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.070 align:middle line:84%
You have to know
exactly what organelle

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:45.840 align:middle line:84%
and what organism
you're dealing with,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.650 align:middle line:84%
but you can apply the same
kind of computer codes

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.680 align:middle line:84%
that we had, a couple
of slides back.

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:54.780 align:middle line:84%
But now we get into
even more complicated.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.090 align:middle line:84%
And part of the reason I'm
showing you this early on,

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:01.500 align:middle line:84%
some of these things would
not be in your textbooks

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:02.940 align:middle line:90%
for the first biology course.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:07.290 align:middle line:84%
And they would not be in a
first computational biology

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:10.080 align:middle line:90%
lecture or two.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.750 align:middle line:84%
But I do this so that you'll
have a healthy distrust

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:15.210 align:middle line:84%
of everything that you read
and everything you hear,

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:18.510 align:middle line:84%
including everything
you hear from me.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:22.770 align:middle line:84%
And this should really make
you distrust the genetic code.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:26.220 align:middle line:84%
Because what these ribosomes
do, in this particular sequence,

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.880 align:middle line:84%
which has been well-documented,
eight years ago,

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.490 align:middle line:84%
is that they will hop
over 50 nucleotides.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:36.360 align:middle line:84%
They're not going 3 nucleotides
at a time as they should,

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.220 align:middle line:84%
in fact, it's not even
an integral multiple

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:39.360 align:middle line:90%
of 3 nucleotides.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.220 align:middle line:84%
It is literally coming
to a stop codon,

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:45.570 align:middle line:84%
and rather than stopping, if
it has just the right sequence

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:49.890 align:middle line:84%
context, including
this complicated RNA

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.810 align:middle line:84%
secondary structure
called a pseudoknot.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.930 align:middle line:84%
The RNA folds up the messenger,
which really should just

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.690 align:middle line:84%
be a messenger, which
the computer should just

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.120 align:middle line:90%
be recognizing--

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.077 align:middle line:84%
or the biological, biochemical
computer, as is the ribosome,

00:15:03.077 --> 00:15:05.160 align:middle line:84%
should be recognizing three
nucleotides at a time,

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.200 align:middle line:84%
instead it's recognizing
a morphology.

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:10.470 align:middle line:84%
This thing folds
up, and no longer

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:11.730 align:middle line:90%
is an informational molecule.

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:14.790 align:middle line:84%
It is a morphological
recognition element.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.290 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, when it
finds that, it skips

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:18.690 align:middle line:84%
over 50 nucleotides,
skips over the stop codon,

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:22.890 align:middle line:84%
and makes a, otherwise,
perfectly normal protein.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:27.930 align:middle line:84%
So don't even trust dogma,
especially don't trust dogma,

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.000 align:middle line:90%
central dogma included.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.880 align:middle line:90%
Plenty of counter-examples.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:39.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're going to move from
this very, very simple example

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:44.430 align:middle line:84%
of an algorithm, where we
can model proteins directly

00:15:44.430 --> 00:15:49.140 align:middle line:84%
from the nucleic acids that come
out of DNA sequences wholesale.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:53.740 align:middle line:84%
We now want to ask, how do we
get the more quantitative data,

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:55.740 align:middle line:84%
which comes out of
functional genomics rather

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.380 align:middle line:90%
than classical sequencing?

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:00.960 align:middle line:84%
How do we get that into
quantitative models,

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.600 align:middle line:84%
and then get the quantitative
models then repopulated

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:09.180 align:middle line:84%
with additional quantitative
data to make a full model?

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.050 align:middle line:84%
Now, in order to
say, came up earlier

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:19.230 align:middle line:84%
as a question, what is the
function of a gene product?

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:23.700 align:middle line:84%
We dip into
qualitative statements

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:26.280 align:middle line:84%
which are made in
the literature, which

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:28.620 align:middle line:84%
have various ways
of representing

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:29.700 align:middle line:90%
the evidence for this.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:32.970 align:middle line:84%
Some of them very convoluted
arguments, some of them

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:35.760 align:middle line:90%
very casual.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.190 align:middle line:84%
But when an attempt
is made to put these

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:41.430 align:middle line:84%
into a database or
a data structure,

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:44.220 align:middle line:84%
as representative,
gross oversimplification

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.505 align:middle line:84%
of the literature, this
is what often comes out,

00:16:46.505 --> 00:16:47.880 align:middle line:84%
something like
this, where you'll

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:49.260 align:middle line:90%
have a hierarchical table.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.000 align:middle line:84%
Here, I've blown up one of
the levels of the hierarchy.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.840 align:middle line:84%
You can think of as a list,
where the list may not

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:57.060 align:middle line:84%
be in a particularly
logical order,

00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:01.190 align:middle line:84%
but the hierarchy is,
so that under metabolism

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:04.250 align:middle line:84%
would be some covalent
change in substrates,

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:08.463 align:middle line:90%
which enzymes would catalyze.

00:17:08.463 --> 00:17:10.130 align:middle line:84%
And then you'd have
information transfer

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:13.075 align:middle line:84%
we've been talking about,
like the DNA to RNA

00:17:13.075 --> 00:17:14.540 align:middle line:90%
protein, these biopolymers.

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:18.200 align:middle line:84%
Regulation of information
transfer or of metabolism

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:21.770 align:middle line:84%
would have all these four
subheadings type of regulation,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.460 align:middle line:90%
trigger, and so on.

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.089 align:middle line:84%
And then transport in these
various other processes.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:29.810 align:middle line:84%
Each of these functions, such as
illustrated by these references

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:35.990 align:middle line:84%
here, can be used in
a way of connecting

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:39.230 align:middle line:84%
all the new information
we get to some

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:47.780 align:middle line:84%
of systematic best guess, best
estimate, of encapsulation

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:49.100 align:middle line:90%
of the literature.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:53.840 align:middle line:84%
Another example of this, in
addition to the MIPS for yeast,

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.400 align:middle line:84%
is gene ontology, which is
derived from the word ontology,

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.960 align:middle line:90%
or nature of being.

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:03.800 align:middle line:84%
And the objective f GO,
abbreviation of Gene Ontology,

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:07.760 align:middle line:84%
is to provide a
controlled vocabulary.

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:09.470 align:middle line:84%
My vocabulary
during this lecture

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:14.000 align:middle line:84%
has been uncontrolled, as
you've probably guessed.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.280 align:middle line:84%
But when you start
talking about--

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:19.160 align:middle line:84%
I have pointed out
the problems that you

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:22.220 align:middle line:84%
get into when you casually
refer to gene expression

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.410 align:middle line:84%
when you really
mean RNA expression,

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.470 align:middle line:84%
and refer to genes as
protein-coding entities,

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:30.200 align:middle line:84%
when you really mean protein
or RNA-encoding entities.

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.378 align:middle line:84%
That process of being more
precise about our use of terms,

00:18:33.378 --> 00:18:35.420 align:middle line:84%
at least when we're
communicating with computers,

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:36.300 align:middle line:90%
is very important.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.883 align:middle line:84%
We communicate with each other,
you guys will give me a little

00:18:38.883 --> 00:18:42.380 align:middle line:84%
slack, some of you
but computers won't.

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.240 align:middle line:84%
They will misinterpret
every chance they get.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:48.900 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what control
of vocabulary is all about.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.550 align:middle line:84%
And you'll have
three different--

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:54.560 align:middle line:84%
Dave, the inventors
of gene ontology,

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.040 align:middle line:84%
have a hierarchy including
molecular function,

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:00.140 align:middle line:84%
biological processes, and
the cellular component,

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:04.290 align:middle line:84%
which we'll expand
upon in the next slide.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.470 align:middle line:84%
Cautionary note, whenever
you do modeling or you

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.630 align:middle line:84%
will be assumptions
in this case.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:12.210 align:middle line:84%
Some of the assumptions
exclude vast parts

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:14.730 align:middle line:84%
of biology, which
are listed here

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:18.210 align:middle line:90%
as part of their documentation.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:23.460 align:middle line:84%
They have things that are not
modeled in the gene ontology

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:26.520 align:middle line:84%
are domain structure and
three-dimensional structure,

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:29.800 align:middle line:84%
which, obviously, has played
a big role in the two lectures

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:31.380 align:middle line:90%
so far.

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:35.850 align:middle line:84%
The evolution and
gene expression, we've

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:39.510 align:middle line:84%
already talked about the
phylogenetic tree of evolution,

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:41.790 align:middle line:84%
and the gene expression
will be a big topic

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:44.910 align:middle line:84%
in the RNA and proteomics
part of this course.

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:47.190 align:middle line:84%
The small molecules
we've illustrated today.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:48.660 align:middle line:84%
Almost everything
in this course,

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.970 align:middle line:84%
seems to be excluded
from the gene ontology.

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:56.610 align:middle line:84%
Nevertheless, here we
go with just one slide

00:19:56.610 --> 00:19:58.560 align:middle line:90%
talking about the functions.

00:19:58.560 --> 00:19:59.910 align:middle line:90%
We have molecular function.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:03.160 align:middle line:84%
What the gene product can
do without specifying where

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:04.440 align:middle line:90%
or when.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:08.220 align:middle line:84%
A broad example of
this, would be enzyme,

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:09.720 align:middle line:90%
something that catalyzes.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.970 align:middle line:84%
And then a very specific
example of an enzyme

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.310 align:middle line:84%
would be, a adenylate
cyclase, something

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:22.210 align:middle line:84%
that makes a cycle in the
ribose of a adenylate.

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:25.410 align:middle line:84%
So both of these fall under
the net molecular function

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:29.280 align:middle line:84%
when you're describing
a function of a protein

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:31.890 align:middle line:90%
in describing a genome.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:35.010 align:middle line:84%
A biological process has
to have more than one step.

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:37.950 align:middle line:84%
If it's one step,
that's not a process.

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:41.130 align:middle line:84%
It has to have a time
component, typically,

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:42.990 align:middle line:84%
and there's a
transformation that occurs.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:46.720 align:middle line:84%
Examples of signal transduction
is a broad biological process.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:48.360 align:middle line:84%
An example of
signal transduction

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:51.600 align:middle line:90%
is cyclic AMP biosynthesis.

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.450 align:middle line:84%
The cellular component,
would be somehow reflecting

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:57.720 align:middle line:84%
this assembly to organelle that
we were talking about earlier.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:01.110 align:middle line:84%
And here, an example, you
have a ribosomal protein

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:03.770 align:middle line:90%
being part of a ribosome.

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:05.520 align:middle line:84%
So it gets you some
idea of the component,

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.860 align:middle line:84%
some molecular-function
biological process

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.090 align:middle line:90%
and components.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:13.980 align:middle line:84%
Now, as I said, this gene
ontology is based on facts.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:18.780 align:middle line:84%
The facts that are
included, it's not--

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.750 align:middle line:84%
ideally, there would be a
direct logical connection

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.300 align:middle line:84%
between the facts that are
summarized in the hierarchical

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.580 align:middle line:84%
gene ontology and
the raw data that

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:27.870 align:middle line:90%
came out of some instrument.

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.042 align:middle line:90%
That is not the case.

00:21:29.042 --> 00:21:30.750 align:middle line:84%
This is all from the
literature, and it's

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.420 align:middle line:90%
done on a low budget, wow.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:37.110 align:middle line:84%
And examples of how
they summarize it,

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.690 align:middle line:84%
is it's inferred from
a mutant phenotype

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:41.230 align:middle line:90%
or a genetic interaction.

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:42.420 align:middle line:90%
So these two are genetic.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:47.670 align:middle line:84%
Or physical interaction,
this passes for biophysics.

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:49.620 align:middle line:84%
Sequence similarity,
now we're starting--

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:51.450 align:middle line:84%
as we go down this
list, we're starting

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:55.350 align:middle line:84%
to get into murkier
and murkier evidence.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:58.740 align:middle line:84%
Sequence similarity, as you'll
see in a subsequence slide,

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:02.700 align:middle line:90%
has problems.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:08.910 align:middle line:84%
A direct assay could be
a physical interaction,

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.670 align:middle line:84%
or it could be some
other biochemical assay.

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:14.880 align:middle line:84%
Expression pattern
might be evidence

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.570 align:middle line:84%
of some of the
associations that were

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:20.790 align:middle line:90%
mentioned in the gene ontology.

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:22.920 align:middle line:84%
Now we get to
electronic annotation.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:25.290 align:middle line:84%
In a certain sense,
all of these things

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:26.785 align:middle line:90%
are electronic annotation.

00:22:26.785 --> 00:22:29.160 align:middle line:84%
Sequence similarity might be
a way that you automatically

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:30.690 align:middle line:90%
get electronic annotation.

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:33.100 align:middle line:84%
Then you get to a
traceable author statement.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:35.880 align:middle line:84%
This means that someone
said something is true,

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:39.360 align:middle line:84%
without saying how he
or she knows it's true,

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:40.680 align:middle line:90%
so we're getting really murky.

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:44.820 align:middle line:84%
And the murkiest of all is
non-traceable authors' data.

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:49.140 align:middle line:84%
You don't even know who said
something might be true, OK.

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:51.180 align:middle line:84%
Let's go back up
to the top of that,

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:52.650 align:middle line:84%
in fact, go beyond
the top of it,

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:57.300 align:middle line:84%
where we now will start tracking
the data from the instruments

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:59.250 align:middle line:90%
to statements.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:02.100 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, in
this course, you

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:09.270 align:middle line:84%
will see how we will, in
the future and present,

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:12.390 align:middle line:84%
make models in a rigorous
way where you can track it

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:13.990 align:middle line:90%
all the way back, to data.

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:18.270 align:middle line:84%
So one class, the most obvious
class, of data collection,

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.790 align:middle line:84%
is what I would call direct
observation, typically

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:22.050 align:middle line:90%
through a microscope.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:24.420 align:middle line:84%
And here's a particularly
powerful case.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.330 align:middle line:84%
I promised you
earlier that we would

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:33.180 align:middle line:84%
talk about how you have 959
cells in the non-gonadal cell

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:34.350 align:middle line:90%
lineage of the worm.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:36.120 align:middle line:84%
It starts as a
single cell up here

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.730 align:middle line:84%
at the top middle,
a fertilized egg,

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.890 align:middle line:84%
as this egg-shaped
thing at the top.

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:45.085 align:middle line:84%
And then it splits off,
way off to the left

00:23:45.085 --> 00:23:45.960 align:middle line:90%
and off to the right.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And that makes two
cells, two stem cells,

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:51.060 align:middle line:84%
that are capable of
differentiating and dividing

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:51.750 align:middle line:90%
further.

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:55.480 align:middle line:84%
And they each make two
more, and it keeps going.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:59.310 align:middle line:84%
But you can see it starts
getting symmetry breaking,

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:00.580 align:middle line:90%
almost immediately.

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:05.320 align:middle line:84%
In fact, the egg itself
is an asymmetric entity.

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.030 align:middle line:84%
And you start
getting lineages that

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:10.300 align:middle line:84%
will either die
as they terminate,

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:12.550 align:middle line:90%
or they will just stop dividing.

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:18.190 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, you end up
with, after about 1,000 cell

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:23.470 align:middle line:84%
divisions or so, you end up with
these 959 non-gonadal cells.

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:26.050 align:middle line:90%


00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:28.780 align:middle line:84%
And this lineage has been
completely mapped out

00:24:28.780 --> 00:24:31.510 align:middle line:84%
by direct microscopic
observation, where

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:33.820 align:middle line:84%
a series of photographs
you can show

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:36.970 align:middle line:84%
that this single cell
turns into these two cells,

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:38.830 align:middle line:84%
so you have a time
axis, and you have

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:44.650 align:middle line:84%
a lineage axis, which is one of
these directed acyclic graphs.

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:47.320 align:middle line:84%
In addition, and
even more amazing,

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.680 align:middle line:84%
to me, anyway, is that you have
a complete neural connection

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.160 align:middle line:90%
for this multicellular organism.

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:55.900 align:middle line:84%
It has a fairly simple brain if
you've ever had a conversation

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:57.010 align:middle line:90%
with one of these things.

00:24:57.010 --> 00:25:09.550 align:middle line:84%
But each neuron can have dozens
to hundreds of connections.

00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:11.830 align:middle line:84%
And these have been
mapped by a serial section

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.830 align:middle line:84%
through the entire
worm, very thin sections

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:16.280 align:middle line:90%
and electron microscopy.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:18.350 align:middle line:84%
And then checking out
the whole wiring diagram.

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:19.900 align:middle line:90%
This is really a tour de force.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:21.580 align:middle line:84%
And part of the
reason it's possible,

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:24.190 align:middle line:84%
this would even be hard to
do in a variety of organisms,

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:26.590 align:middle line:84%
but this is another case
where biology cooperates,

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:30.190 align:middle line:84%
just like with the genetic code,
in this particular organism,

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:34.120 align:middle line:84%
that lineage happens
the same way every time.

00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:37.180 align:middle line:84%
In even slightly more
complicated organisms,

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:40.870 align:middle line:84%
like the Drosophila
fruit fly or humans.

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:45.460 align:middle line:84%
The lineages are not
so strict, and a cell

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:48.100 align:middle line:84%
can take on a number
of different directions

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:52.300 align:middle line:84%
depending on the exact physical
environment it finds itself in.

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:54.850 align:middle line:84%
But nevertheless, for this
one, the neural connections

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:57.550 align:middle line:84%
are reproducible, and the
cell lineage is reproducible.

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:00.100 align:middle line:90%
And so you can map this all out.

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:02.710 align:middle line:84%
For other organisms, it doesn't
mean you shouldn't try it,

00:26:02.710 --> 00:26:05.002 align:middle line:84%
it just means you'll have to
represent it in a slightly

00:26:05.002 --> 00:26:07.240 align:middle line:90%
less-fixed pattern.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:10.420 align:middle line:84%
You'll have to represent it as
probabilistic set of divisions

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:12.610 align:middle line:84%
and probabilistic set
of neural connections.

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:17.500 align:middle line:84%
And maybe even, conditional,
on various conditions.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:19.840 align:middle line:84%
OK, that's direct
observation as a class

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:23.470 align:middle line:90%
of source of data for modeling.

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:25.810 align:middle line:84%
Here's three other
sources of data.

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:28.930 align:middle line:84%
In each case, I've shown
pretty raw representations

00:26:28.930 --> 00:26:30.160 align:middle line:90%
of the data.

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:33.070 align:middle line:84%
You can think of these all
as representing an intensity

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:38.200 align:middle line:84%
readout, with some
sort of separation,

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:44.570 align:middle line:84%
as the horizontal axis or
is some cases, both axes.

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:46.810 align:middle line:84%
So the intensity
here, is indicated

00:26:46.810 --> 00:26:50.140 align:middle line:84%
by a line plot of
four different color

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:54.550 align:middle line:84%
fluorophores in an
electrophoretic separation,

00:26:54.550 --> 00:26:56.800 align:middle line:84%
which is the basis for
the genomic sequencing

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:00.880 align:middle line:90%
that we're so proud of here.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:06.070 align:middle line:84%
Then so here, the detection
of this fluorescence

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:09.940 align:middle line:84%
of the terminated
chains of DNA, we'll

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:11.890 align:middle line:90%
get to that later in the course.

00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:13.510 align:middle line:84%
Here you have mass
spectrometry where

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:16.570 align:middle line:84%
you're measuring
differences in masses,

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.910 align:middle line:84%
even more accurately
than in sequencing.

00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:21.370 align:middle line:84%
You're separating nucleic
acids by their differences

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:24.070 align:middle line:84%
in mass, so about
1 part in 1,000.

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:26.710 align:middle line:84%
The mass spectrometry is
more like 1 part in 10,000,

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:28.780 align:middle line:90%
or even better.

00:27:28.780 --> 00:27:31.480 align:middle line:84%
Because here, you're
separating in a gas phase,

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:34.780 align:middle line:84%
based on the electrical
and magnetic properties.

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:37.450 align:middle line:84%
Here, you're
separating by charge

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:42.610 align:middle line:84%
in a liquid phase,
liquid and gel phase.

00:27:42.610 --> 00:27:46.570 align:middle line:84%
Each of these, you can
specify the throughput per day

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.360 align:middle line:84%
or the throughput
per unit dollar.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:52.390 align:middle line:84%
This becomes important in
planning these structures.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:56.590 align:middle line:84%
The third category
here, is arrays.

00:27:56.590 --> 00:28:00.670 align:middle line:84%
These can be arrays of nucleic
acids for quantitating RNAs,

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:04.450 align:middle line:84%
or arrays of
antibodies, proteins,

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:08.050 align:middle line:84%
small-molecule chemicals,
which we can quantitate

00:28:08.050 --> 00:28:10.030 align:middle line:84%
the binding of one
kind of molecule

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:13.090 align:middle line:90%
to an array of other molecules.

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:16.810 align:middle line:84%
In both the top and the bottom,
you can have multiple colors.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:19.720 align:middle line:84%
And these can be
used quantitatively

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:23.512 align:middle line:84%
as internal standards so that
you can monitor this process.

00:28:23.512 --> 00:28:25.970 align:middle line:84%
See, we're going to go into
this in great detail, later on.

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:27.340 align:middle line:84%
But I wanted to give
you a feeling for where

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:28.632 align:middle line:90%
the source of these things are.

00:28:28.632 --> 00:28:31.120 align:middle line:84%
This array analysis, in a
sense, is another example

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:31.780 align:middle line:90%
of microscopy.

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:33.238 align:middle line:84%
Just like in the
previous slide, we

00:28:33.238 --> 00:28:34.840 align:middle line:84%
used direct
observation microscopy

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:37.010 align:middle line:90%
to monitor cell lineages.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.535 align:middle line:84%
So too we can do
it/ We can make a--

00:28:39.535 --> 00:28:43.067 align:middle line:90%


00:28:43.067 --> 00:28:43.900 align:middle line:90%
it's just wonderful.

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:45.340 align:middle line:90%
The battery's charged, OK.

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:53.070 align:middle line:90%


00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:57.930 align:middle line:84%
We can take the microscopy
of artificial patterns,

00:28:57.930 --> 00:28:59.730 align:middle line:90%
such as arrays.

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.760 align:middle line:84%
Just as we have
separation here by mass,

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.340 align:middle line:84%
we can also have separation on
a variety of other properties,

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:08.040 align:middle line:84%
sometimes called
multidimensional separation.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:12.840 align:middle line:84%
This gets back to the first
slide of the lecture, which

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:15.870 align:middle line:90%
was the purification aspect.

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:19.440 align:middle line:84%
Now, how do we jump from
that kind of raw data

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:24.570 align:middle line:84%
to this common way that
biologists communicate

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:27.240 align:middle line:84%
in journals, where they have
circles and arrows, where

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:29.280 align:middle line:84%
the circles might be
some kind of protein

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:33.300 align:middle line:84%
molecule such as
a stat, and arrow

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.210 align:middle line:84%
indicates some of
interaction, or regulation,

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:39.810 align:middle line:84%
or quantitative influence
that one protein

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:43.770 align:middle line:90%
has on another, or a protein.

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:48.150 align:middle line:84%
So in alternative diagrams,
nodes could be small molecules,

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:51.570 align:middle line:84%
and the edges, the
links between the nodes,

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:54.900 align:middle line:84%
could be an enzymatic reaction
catalyzed by a protein.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:57.570 align:middle line:84%
There are about 500
biological databases

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:01.230 align:middle line:84%
that we'll talk about
in the database talk.

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:04.230 align:middle line:84%
How the data and models were
entered into these databases,

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.770 align:middle line:90%
is a huge issue.

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:10.050 align:middle line:84%
Many of them have been
done very casually.

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:14.050 align:middle line:84%
For DNA sequencing
and crystallography,

00:30:14.050 --> 00:30:16.510 align:middle line:84%
I think the process by which
you go from the raw data

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:19.000 align:middle line:84%
to the models, is very
well understood, very well

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:21.520 align:middle line:84%
communicated, for
this sort of thing.

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:24.820 align:middle line:84%
It will take this whole course
for us to even to scratch

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:25.970 align:middle line:90%
the surface.

00:30:25.970 --> 00:30:27.550 align:middle line:84%
Here's another
example-- that one was

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:29.480 align:middle line:90%
protein-protein interactions.

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:32.530 align:middle line:84%
This is an example where
the nodes now are not

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:34.240 align:middle line:90%
proteins but small molecules.

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:36.730 align:middle line:84%
And they're connected
by an enzymatic pathway.

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:39.370 align:middle line:84%
This is another example
of an application

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:41.380 align:middle line:84%
of ordinary
differentiable equations,

00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:43.630 align:middle line:90%
just like the one last class.

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.610 align:middle line:90%
We had exponential growth.

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:49.410 align:middle line:84%
Here, you have
simple fluxes, where

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:54.010 align:middle line:84%
a catalytic reaction occurs, not
autocatalytic, but catalytic.

00:30:54.010 --> 00:30:56.470 align:middle line:84%
There's no there's no
exponential growth occurring

00:30:56.470 --> 00:30:58.600 align:middle line:90%
in this cell.

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:03.550 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have any biopolymers
in it, biopolymer synthesis.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:07.000 align:middle line:84%
But these catalytic reactions
that form this network,

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:11.260 align:middle line:84%
and you can model the
influx of fresh molecules.

00:31:11.260 --> 00:31:15.980 align:middle line:84%
And its utilization within
the cell and the efflux.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:17.510 align:middle line:90%
We'll come back to that.

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:20.810 align:middle line:84%
Inside there, are a set
of kinetic equations.

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:23.150 align:middle line:84%
We need to figure out how to
get from the raw data types

00:31:23.150 --> 00:31:25.890 align:middle line:84%
that I've shown you to
this kind of equation.

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:28.190 align:middle line:84%
This will be one of the
goals of the course.

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:31.670 align:middle line:84%
Here, you have a velocity
on the far-left hand

00:31:31.670 --> 00:31:34.460 align:middle line:84%
side of the top
equation, which is

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:39.230 align:middle line:84%
related to a maximum
velocity on the numerator.

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:44.300 align:middle line:84%
And then a series of
linear sums and quotients.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:48.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, some of the terms
will be nonlinear.

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:50.960 align:middle line:84%
Here's an exponent
of 4 that enters in,

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.307 align:middle line:84%
because you have one of these
[? propertivities ?] that

00:31:53.307 --> 00:31:54.890 align:middle line:84%
gives you that kind
of sigmoidal curve

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:57.260 align:middle line:84%
that we showed for
transistors and will enter

00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:59.810 align:middle line:84%
to a number of
biological consequences,

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:02.180 align:middle line:84%
where the steepness of
that sigmoidal curve

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:04.610 align:middle line:84%
is determined by this exponent,
sometimes called a Hill

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:06.500 align:middle line:90%
coefficient.

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:10.580 align:middle line:84%
But other than that, you'll
get these simple, linear sums

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:11.630 align:middle line:90%
and quotients.

00:32:11.630 --> 00:32:14.120 align:middle line:90%
And we'll come back to that.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:17.990 align:middle line:84%
What actually constitutes
these networks?

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:21.860 align:middle line:84%
I want you to feel
less limited than you

00:32:21.860 --> 00:32:24.140 align:middle line:90%
might get in a simple textbook.

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:27.860 align:middle line:84%
A simple, textbook definition
of a catalytic enzyme-catalyzed

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:31.820 align:middle line:84%
process you might
have, A is a substrate

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:33.590 align:middle line:90%
that turns into B as a product.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:36.920 align:middle line:84%
This is a process that A
could go to B spontaneously,

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.197 align:middle line:84%
but in the presence of
enzyme, it goes faster.

00:32:39.197 --> 00:32:41.280 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be, that for
all intents and purposes,

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:44.420 align:middle line:84%
A never turns into B.
It's so slow that you

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.437 align:middle line:84%
need this enzyme here
to even detect it.

00:32:46.437 --> 00:32:48.020 align:middle line:84%
The enzyme will form
a complex with A.

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:51.380 align:middle line:84%
This could be a non-covalent
complex or a covalent one.

00:32:51.380 --> 00:32:54.200 align:middle line:84%
It then produces
a covalent change

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:59.120 align:middle line:84%
in A. Ant it becomes an
enzyme-bound B. B is released.

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:01.940 align:middle line:90%
Enzyme E is regenerated.

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:05.330 align:middle line:84%
And so in a certain sense, in
this process of turning A to B,

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.610 align:middle line:90%
E is not consumed.

00:33:07.610 --> 00:33:11.390 align:middle line:84%
But let's think about an
increasingly important class

00:33:11.390 --> 00:33:14.300 align:middle line:84%
of biochemistry, such as
signal transduction, where

00:33:14.300 --> 00:33:16.250 align:middle line:90%
the enzyme now has a new role.

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:18.620 align:middle line:84%
It changes places
with the substrate.

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.300 align:middle line:90%
It becomes a substrate.

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:25.280 align:middle line:84%
The E now is a substrate in
which a small molecule, ATP,

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:31.370 align:middle line:84%
which might have been the A
up here, combines with the E.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:35.930 align:middle line:84%
And the E could either catalyze
its own phosphorylation

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.910 align:middle line:84%
or in context with another
enzyme, but in any case,

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:41.330 align:middle line:84%
it becomes covalently
modified to produce

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:44.360 align:middle line:84%
a phosphorylated enzyme,
a phosphorylated protein.

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:48.630 align:middle line:84%
And then the ATP is regenerated
by a simple enzymatic process.

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:50.630 align:middle line:84%
And so in a certain
sense, formally, it's

00:33:50.630 --> 00:33:53.150 align:middle line:84%
very similar to this
process, except you now flip

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:54.380 align:middle line:90%
the enzyme and the substrate.

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:58.070 align:middle line:84%
ATP is not consumed, the small
molecule is not consumed.

00:33:58.070 --> 00:33:59.450 align:middle line:90%
The enzyme is consumed.

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:03.140 align:middle line:84%
So think of these things, these
networks, as symmetrically

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:04.280 align:middle line:90%
as you can.

00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:09.248 align:middle line:84%
Try not to get too embedded in
the names, this is an enzyme,

00:34:09.248 --> 00:34:11.540 align:middle line:84%
this is a substrate, and
think more about the concepts.

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:14.510 align:middle line:84%
The concept here, is
some things are consumed

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:20.469 align:middle line:84%
and some things are
catalytic and regenerated.

00:34:20.469 --> 00:34:22.690 align:middle line:84%
So again, we are
going to integrate

00:34:22.690 --> 00:34:24.969 align:middle line:84%
this metabolic processes
we were talking about,

00:34:24.969 --> 00:34:28.570 align:middle line:84%
in the last couple of
slides, with the information

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:33.370 align:middle line:84%
flow, which was the
topic of central dogma,

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:37.150 align:middle line:84%
in order to get
functional genomics,

00:34:37.150 --> 00:34:41.679 align:middle line:84%
which measures those information
molecules mostly and produces

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:43.730 align:middle line:90%
quantitative modeling.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:45.909 align:middle line:84%
You need to have the
qualitative models to know

00:34:45.909 --> 00:34:47.409 align:middle line:90%
what's connected to what.

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:50.530 align:middle line:84%
You need to have the raw data
as illustrated in slide 41.

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:54.050 align:middle line:84%
Again, to remind you, the
source of quantitative data

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:57.940 align:middle line:84%
here, you can measure RNA,
or proteins, or peptides

00:34:57.940 --> 00:34:59.350 align:middle line:90%
in the mass spectrometry.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:02.620 align:middle line:84%
RNA in the arrays
connected to the DNA

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:05.410 align:middle line:90%
provided by the DNA sequencing.

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:11.560 align:middle line:84%
I warned you that one of
the gene ontology data type

00:35:11.560 --> 00:35:15.790 align:middle line:84%
sources of data was sequenced--
electronic sequence annotation

00:35:15.790 --> 00:35:17.590 align:middle line:90%
by sequence similarity.

00:35:17.590 --> 00:35:20.830 align:middle line:84%
I want to elaborate
on this warning

00:35:20.830 --> 00:35:27.460 align:middle line:84%
with this slide, where we say,
we have various justifications

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:30.550 align:middle line:84%
for looking for distant
homologs, examples

00:35:30.550 --> 00:35:34.780 align:middle line:84%
of gene products,
which are related by,

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:37.960 align:middle line:84%
on that ultimate
pedigree tree of life,

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:39.710 align:middle line:90%
by very long distances.

00:35:39.710 --> 00:35:41.770 align:middle line:84%
It's been a long time
since those things were

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:43.667 align:middle line:90%
present as a common ancestor.

00:35:43.667 --> 00:35:45.250 align:middle line:84%
And we want to find
those because they

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:48.130 align:middle line:84%
help us limit the
number of hypotheses

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:51.080 align:middle line:84%
that we need to test whenever
we find a new molecule.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:53.230 align:middle line:84%
If we can connect it
to another molecule,

00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:56.155 align:middle line:84%
however distant,
then we feel that we

00:35:56.155 --> 00:35:58.030 align:middle line:84%
don't have to test every
possible hypothesis.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:36:00.070 align:middle line:84%
We just have to test
that little narrow one.

00:36:00.070 --> 00:36:02.980 align:middle line:84%
But what happens
when we do that?

00:36:02.980 --> 00:36:04.990 align:middle line:84%
Let's say, instead of
some distant homology,

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:07.930 align:middle line:84%
where we have, say 20%
amino acid identity.

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:09.610 align:middle line:84%
You line up the
sequences by methods

00:36:09.610 --> 00:36:11.000 align:middle line:90%
that we'll talk about later.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:13.570 align:middle line:84%
And you have 20%
of the positions

00:36:13.570 --> 00:36:19.100 align:middle line:84%
that are the same, or even less,
can sometimes be meaningful.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:20.710 align:middle line:90%
But how good is that?

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:22.630 align:middle line:84%
There's going to be
some kind of curve that

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:26.860 align:middle line:84%
relates how close two proteins
are with a probability

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:30.580 align:middle line:84%
that they will have the
same biochemical, or cell

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:33.550 align:middle line:90%
biological, or genetic function.

00:36:33.550 --> 00:36:35.620 align:middle line:84%
And here's some
worst case scenarios.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:37.810 align:middle line:84%
And I don't mean to
represent these as typical,

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:40.360 align:middle line:84%
but they get you
doubting again so

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:43.990 align:middle line:90%
that you don't trust anything.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:45.460 align:middle line:90%
100% sequence identity.

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:48.850 align:middle line:84%
This should be a best case
scenario, but it's not.

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:52.600 align:middle line:84%
The amylase enzyme, which
catalyzes a carbon metabolism

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:55.450 align:middle line:84%
in most cells when it's
expressed to high levels

00:36:55.450 --> 00:37:01.810 align:middle line:84%
in a vertebrate like our
friend this marine tortoise,

00:37:01.810 --> 00:37:04.592 align:middle line:84%
turtle, it turns into the
major eye lens protein.

00:37:04.592 --> 00:37:06.550 align:middle line:84%
And actually, this is
true of most vertebrates.

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:09.520 align:middle line:84%
They have some kind of enzyme,
like a glycolic enzyme,

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:12.700 align:middle line:84%
which is overproduced
and aggregates

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:17.170 align:middle line:84%
and makes a clear lens
more morphologically

00:37:17.170 --> 00:37:19.690 align:middle line:84%
interesting feature,
which just focuses light.

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:22.900 align:middle line:84%
Completely new function by all
those definitions of function.

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:24.820 align:middle line:84%
No longer does the
enzymatic activity,

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:27.130 align:middle line:84%
does an optical
activity instead.

00:37:27.130 --> 00:37:29.320 align:middle line:84%
Another example, we have
100% sequence identity.

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:32.500 align:middle line:84%
Not something really distant
homolog like 20% or 10%,

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:34.480 align:middle line:90%
but 100% sequence identity.

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:37.120 align:middle line:84%
[? Thyroxine, ?] which is
involved in redox reactions

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:39.640 align:middle line:84%
involving [INAUDIBLE]
and other things.

00:37:39.640 --> 00:37:41.740 align:middle line:84%
In the right context
with other proteins,

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:44.110 align:middle line:84%
it can now be part
of a DNA polymerase,

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:48.010 align:middle line:84%
when it globs on the DNA, it
goes really without stopping

00:37:48.010 --> 00:37:51.340 align:middle line:84%
with [? thyroxine, ?] but it
falls off if [? thyroxine ?]

00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:52.060 align:middle line:90%
isn't around.

00:37:52.060 --> 00:37:54.400 align:middle line:84%
That's not a redox function
completely different

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:56.927 align:middle line:90%
biochemical function.

00:37:56.927 --> 00:37:58.510 align:middle line:84%
But like I say, there
will be a curve.

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:03.580 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, there will
be very great hypothesis

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:07.150 align:middle line:84%
limitation that can come
from very distant relatives.

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:09.840 align:middle line:90%


00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:15.750 align:middle line:84%
These are more examples
of the quantitative data

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:19.380 align:middle line:84%
that we will use to get hints
at relationships among genes

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:21.330 align:middle line:90%
that go up and down together.

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:24.680 align:middle line:90%


00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:28.090 align:middle line:84%
They form the basis
of asking, what

00:38:28.090 --> 00:38:30.820 align:middle line:84%
is function not based
just on sequence homology,

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:33.940 align:middle line:84%
but based on a variety
of quantitative data,

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:38.170 align:middle line:84%
such as the RNA data
and the microarrays.

00:38:38.170 --> 00:38:42.160 align:middle line:84%
This is three more
ways of looking

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:44.260 align:middle line:90%
at how we define functions.

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:46.810 align:middle line:84%
Function definition
number one, is the effects

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:47.890 align:middle line:90%
of mutation on fitness.

00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:49.540 align:middle line:84%
This is, in a
certain sense, what

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:54.640 align:middle line:84%
the organism cares about the
function of a gene product.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:58.330 align:middle line:84%
It's how many grandchildren
am I going to have?

00:38:58.330 --> 00:38:59.960 align:middle line:90%
That's what it cares about.

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:02.662 align:middle line:84%
And that's what shaped
the function over time.

00:39:02.662 --> 00:39:04.120 align:middle line:84%
And so if we're
going to understand

00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:06.520 align:middle line:84%
any of our other
definition functions,

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:09.880 align:middle line:84%
we have to at least
give some attention

00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:12.790 align:middle line:84%
to what shaped it
over billions of years

00:39:12.790 --> 00:39:14.410 align:middle line:84%
and over many
different environments.

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:18.790 align:middle line:84%
We need to have some feeling for
the ecology of these organisms.

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:22.390 align:middle line:84%
The second definition is
the more commonly used one,

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:25.750 align:middle line:84%
which is what is
actually its function

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:27.970 align:middle line:90%
in a machine-like sense.

00:39:27.970 --> 00:39:32.135 align:middle line:84%
In the cogs, in the wheels, how
does it function structurally?

00:39:32.135 --> 00:39:33.760 align:middle line:84%
What's the
three-dimensional structure?

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:35.350 align:middle line:90%
What's the mechanism?

00:39:35.350 --> 00:39:38.470 align:middle line:84%
The third function, is
more forward-looking,

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:41.770 align:middle line:84%
not what good has it been
to organisms in the past,

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:43.810 align:middle line:84%
but what good can it
be to us in the future

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:45.940 align:middle line:84%
or to other organisms
in the future?

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:49.870 align:middle line:84%
This may not involve
reproducing the organism,

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:50.710 align:middle line:90%
making copies of it.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:53.320 align:middle line:84%
It could be that there's
some other engineering goal

00:39:53.320 --> 00:39:56.050 align:middle line:90%
or objective function.

00:39:56.050 --> 00:39:58.120 align:middle line:84%
When we say that we've
proven something.

00:39:58.120 --> 00:40:01.930 align:middle line:84%
We've proven a
biological hypothesis.

00:40:01.930 --> 00:40:04.510 align:middle line:84%
What we mean is,
given the assumption,

00:40:04.510 --> 00:40:06.580 align:middle line:84%
it's a statistical
statement that the odds

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:09.070 align:middle line:84%
of the hypothesis being
wrong are less than 5%

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:13.240 align:middle line:84%
of the time keeping in
mind hidden hypotheses

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:14.260 align:middle line:90%
and multiple hypotheses.

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:16.810 align:middle line:84%
In genomics, it's all too
easy to collect a lot of data,

00:40:16.810 --> 00:40:19.090 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, when
you mine the data,

00:40:19.090 --> 00:40:20.920 align:middle line:84%
you can make a
lot of hypotheses.

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:23.950 align:middle line:84%
And you test them, and you
find you will find thousands

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:25.540 align:middle line:84%
of things, which
by themselves would

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:30.670 align:middle line:84%
be significant at the 5% level,
the standard statistical test,

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:34.810 align:middle line:84%
but you've got to correct
for the number of hypotheses

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:37.210 align:middle line:84%
you implicitly or
explicitly test.

00:40:37.210 --> 00:40:40.492 align:middle line:84%
We'll mention this time
and again in specific cases

00:40:40.492 --> 00:40:41.200 align:middle line:90%
as we go forward.

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:43.770 align:middle line:90%


00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:48.600 align:middle line:84%
The systems biology manifesto
that I mentioned earlier,

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:51.150 align:middle line:84%
had this little loop where you
would generate perturbations

00:40:51.150 --> 00:40:52.660 align:middle line:90%
and test things and so forth.

00:40:52.660 --> 00:40:54.960 align:middle line:84%
But an alternative
way rather than doing

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:57.240 align:middle line:84%
additional experiments,
is if you really

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:59.287 align:middle line:84%
have bought-in fully
into systems biology

00:40:59.287 --> 00:41:00.870 align:middle line:84%
and you really have
all the components

00:41:00.870 --> 00:41:03.210 align:middle line:84%
and a systematic
perturbations, then

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:05.580 align:middle line:84%
you might be able to test
the hypothesis generated

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:08.520 align:middle line:84%
by data mining one data set by
going into another data set.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.780 align:middle line:84%
You need to ensure that
they are independent.

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:15.643 align:middle line:84%
And you need to ensure that
the hypothesis itself, came

00:41:15.643 --> 00:41:18.060 align:middle line:84%
from the first data set and
not the second when you go out

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:18.640 align:middle line:90%
and test it.

00:41:18.640 --> 00:41:21.990 align:middle line:84%
But that would be a pure data
mining loop, systems biology

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:22.890 align:middle line:90%
loop.

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:29.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, just like when we
say we have a proof,

00:41:29.970 --> 00:41:31.920 align:middle line:84%
you should be distrusting
of anybody that

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:33.180 align:middle line:90%
says I have an absolute proof.

00:41:33.180 --> 00:41:36.220 align:middle line:84%
What they really mean, is
a statistical statement.

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:38.352 align:middle line:84%
So too, when someone
says, when they

00:41:38.352 --> 00:41:39.810 align:middle line:84%
refer to the quality
of their data,

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:45.210 align:middle line:84%
is this is the answer at the
raw data level, what they really

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:47.520 align:middle line:84%
mean, is that they
have some error

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:49.950 align:middle line:90%
level that they can quantitate.

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:52.770 align:middle line:84%
And you should be
especially distrustful

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:57.750 align:middle line:84%
if someone doesn't attempt to
give you any feeling for that.

00:41:57.750 --> 00:42:00.600 align:middle line:84%
Not to say that everybody
that gives error bars or error

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:04.300 align:middle line:84%
estimates is to be trusted,
but you get the idea.

00:42:04.300 --> 00:42:07.410 align:middle line:84%
So for DNA sequencing, there's
a standard of practice.

00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:12.390 align:middle line:84%
It was not always such, but
a meeting in Bermuda, it's

00:42:12.390 --> 00:42:14.130 align:middle line:84%
called the Bermuda
standard, this

00:42:14.130 --> 00:42:23.100 align:middle line:84%
is the best place to establish
standards, is 99.99% accurate.

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:25.620 align:middle line:84%
You can see they have very
high standards in Bermuda.

00:42:25.620 --> 00:42:28.350 align:middle line:84%
But that's across
the Genome Project.

00:42:28.350 --> 00:42:31.540 align:middle line:90%


00:42:31.540 --> 00:42:35.250 align:middle line:84%
These are aspects that, I
think, we got from genomics,

00:42:35.250 --> 00:42:39.310 align:middle line:84%
in addition to the raw data,
we've got kind of an attitude.

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:42.900 align:middle line:84%
The attitude is, we can start
looking at whole systems again,

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:47.040 align:middle line:84%
less on the individual
gene-hypothesis driven

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:51.120 align:middle line:84%
standard NIH grant proposals
that predated the Genome

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:51.690 align:middle line:90%
Project.

00:42:51.690 --> 00:42:54.300 align:middle line:84%
Now you can do less
hypothesis driven,

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:58.090 align:middle line:84%
you can do data
mining, and so on.

00:42:58.090 --> 00:43:02.130 align:middle line:84%
We've also inherited the concept
of automation the modeling

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:03.450 align:middle line:90%
and completion.

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:06.447 align:middle line:84%
Completion is something
which still is not

00:43:06.447 --> 00:43:08.280 align:middle line:84%
reduced to practice for
functional genomics,

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:11.050 align:middle line:84%
but it has been reduced to
practice for sequencing.

00:43:11.050 --> 00:43:13.890 align:middle line:84%
And there is hope that
we can approach it

00:43:13.890 --> 00:43:15.510 align:middle line:90%
for functional genomics.

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:17.910 align:middle line:84%
Be careful using
the word impossible.

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:19.710 align:middle line:84%
There certainly are
things that appear

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:21.930 align:middle line:84%
not to be cost effective
at any given moment,

00:43:21.930 --> 00:43:24.780 align:middle line:84%
but technology is
moving quickly enough.

00:43:24.780 --> 00:43:27.510 align:middle line:84%
Remember those greater
than exponential curves

00:43:27.510 --> 00:43:29.490 align:middle line:90%
in the last lecture.

00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:33.600 align:middle line:84%
There are technologies arriving
that make things suddenly

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:36.340 align:middle line:90%
become cost effective.

00:43:36.340 --> 00:43:40.320 align:middle line:84%
And that's a particularly
important warning when you're

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:42.390 align:middle line:84%
designing a
computational method that

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:45.190 align:middle line:84%
will compete with an
experimental method,

00:43:45.190 --> 00:43:47.920 align:middle line:84%
the experimental method
suddenly becomes cost effective,

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:53.310 align:middle line:84%
then you need to revise
your computational goals.

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:56.880 align:middle line:84%
We have types of mutations
that we've talked about.

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:59.970 align:middle line:84%
We have a null mutation, for
example, phenylketonuria,

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:05.080 align:middle line:84%
which is tested in newborns,
in almost all newborns,

00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:07.080 align:middle line:84%
that are born in the
United States and certainly

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:09.750 align:middle line:90%
Massachusetts.

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:13.050 align:middle line:84%
This is a very serious
source of mental retardation

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:15.120 align:middle line:90%
completely wiping out that gene.

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:18.630 align:middle line:84%
Small dosage effects, like a
1.5-fold effect that we talked

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:22.330 align:middle line:84%
about in trisomies, like
Down syndrome, are important.

00:44:22.330 --> 00:44:23.610 align:middle line:90%
You have conditional mutants.

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:27.150 align:middle line:84%
Classically temperature
sensitivity of a mutation,

00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:28.770 align:middle line:90%
meaning the protein unfolds.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:34.560 align:middle line:84%
Or more recent
enthusiasm for chemicals,

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:38.850 align:middle line:84%
a mutation which depends
upon a chemical for producing

00:44:38.850 --> 00:44:40.135 align:middle line:90%
its phenotype.

00:44:40.135 --> 00:44:41.760 align:middle line:84%
You can not only have
these things that

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:48.870 align:middle line:84%
affect dosage or condition
or complete knockout,

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:51.060 align:middle line:84%
you can have a new
function to obtain

00:44:51.060 --> 00:44:52.620 align:middle line:84%
for changing the
ligand specificity

00:44:52.620 --> 00:44:54.900 align:middle line:84%
or changing the
aggregation of a protein.

00:44:54.900 --> 00:44:58.800 align:middle line:84%
Here in the background, are
how a change in the hemoglobin,

00:44:58.800 --> 00:45:00.270 align:middle line:84%
which normally
transports oxygen,

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:04.320 align:middle line:84%
can change the morphology of
a cell and hence the function

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:06.720 align:middle line:90%
in transporting oxygen.

00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:09.870 align:middle line:84%
I just want to end
on two slides on how

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:17.670 align:middle line:84%
you can represent the
competition among cells

00:45:17.670 --> 00:45:20.430 align:middle line:84%
or among organisms,
which represents

00:45:20.430 --> 00:45:24.060 align:middle line:84%
the Darwinian function, function
number one, a few slides back.

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:27.360 align:middle line:84%
Here you have mutants
in a population.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:30.090 align:middle line:84%
Selection acts on
populations, and mutations

00:45:30.090 --> 00:45:33.930 align:middle line:84%
are tagged, by definition,
by their nucleic acid.

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:42.300 align:middle line:84%
If you can use the tags, you
make a pool of such mutants,

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:44.263 align:middle line:84%
these are naturally
occurring population.

00:45:44.263 --> 00:45:45.680 align:middle line:84%
And when these
pools are subjected

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:49.340 align:middle line:84%
to selections natural,
or complex, or simple,

00:45:49.340 --> 00:45:54.560 align:middle line:84%
or in the laboratory you can
now read out these tags in many

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:57.590 align:middle line:84%
of the quantitative ways we
talked about, for instance,

00:45:57.590 --> 00:46:00.450 align:middle line:84%
mass spectrometry,
arrays, so on.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:06.433 align:middle line:84%
And as you go through
more rounds of selection,

00:46:06.433 --> 00:46:07.850 align:middle line:84%
you'll eventually
pick the winner,

00:46:07.850 --> 00:46:10.220 align:middle line:84%
which is the most
highly selected

00:46:10.220 --> 00:46:14.960 align:middle line:90%
of the mutations, the winner.

00:46:14.960 --> 00:46:16.383 align:middle line:84%
Or you might have
a mixture if you

00:46:16.383 --> 00:46:18.050 align:middle line:84%
through a very limited
number of rounds.

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:19.970 align:middle line:84%
This will follow the
exponential curve

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:22.430 align:middle line:84%
that we had here, whether
it's exponential decay

00:46:22.430 --> 00:46:23.690 align:middle line:90%
or exponential growth.

00:46:23.690 --> 00:46:26.420 align:middle line:84%
You can have a very subtle
difference in growth,

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:31.100 align:middle line:84%
due to the function of
that mutated gene product,

00:46:31.100 --> 00:46:36.230 align:middle line:84%
but that small, say
1% turns into complete

00:46:36.230 --> 00:46:39.887 align:middle line:84%
all or none replacement if
you have enough generations.

00:46:39.887 --> 00:46:41.720 align:middle line:84%
This is the awesome
power of the exponential

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:43.025 align:middle line:90%
that we talked about last time.

00:46:43.025 --> 00:46:45.710 align:middle line:90%


00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:50.030 align:middle line:84%
And in real world, and
also in the laboratory,

00:46:50.030 --> 00:46:53.060 align:middle line:84%
you can think of this as going
over a variety of environments,

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:55.130 align:middle line:90%
E, over different times.

00:46:55.130 --> 00:47:00.050 align:middle line:84%
So the time you spend in each
of these different environments,

00:47:00.050 --> 00:47:03.710 align:middle line:90%
has some unit that happens.

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:06.230 align:middle line:84%
In natural environment,
you'll spend say, more time

00:47:06.230 --> 00:47:07.950 align:middle line:90%
in one condition than another.

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And the selection
coefficients are a simple sum,

00:47:11.090 --> 00:47:14.990 align:middle line:84%
and this exponential gives you
the ratio of the organisms.

00:47:14.990 --> 00:47:17.310 align:middle line:90%
Here's some references on this.

00:47:17.310 --> 00:47:21.140 align:middle line:84%
And I urge you to take a look at
these, where actual experiments

00:47:21.140 --> 00:47:22.910 align:middle line:90%
have been done getting these.

00:47:22.910 --> 00:47:26.760 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come back to
this later in the course.

00:47:26.760 --> 00:47:31.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is the end of
this lecture number two.

00:47:31.070 --> 00:47:33.085 align:middle line:90%
Thank you very much.

00:47:33.085 --> 00:48:14.000 align:middle line:90%